% ============================================================================
% soil-horizons — the master soil horizons (O, A, E, B, C, R) and what its
% source says each one IS or is made of (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% Dig a pit straight down through a soil and the wall shows horizontal layers,
% like the layers of a cake. Each layer is a "horizon", and soil scientists give
% the master horizons single capital letters, from the surface down: O, A, E, B,
% C, and then R. A soil-science (or agriculture) agent reasons UP from this rung:
% before you can name a soil, judge its drainage, or plan land use, you must
% first know WHICH horizon you are looking at and what it is made of. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? horizon_material(c, $Material)      % parent_material
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `horizon_material(horizon, material)` carrying the table's citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% material WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these master horizons (it never invents a material).
%
% Each master horizon and the material / thing the source states it is, top to
% bottom of the soil profile, as a little truth table:
%
%     horizon   what the source says it is    a beginner's cue
%     -------   ---------------------------   ----------------------------------
%     o         organic_matter                the leaf-litter layer on top
%     a         topsoil                        the dark, life-rich surface layer
%     e         losses                         the leached, washed-out pale layer
%     b         illuviation                    the subsoil where stuff accumulates
%     c         parent_material                the little-altered material below
%     r         bedrock                        the hard rock at the bottom
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a material and recall its horizon:
%
%     ? horizon_material($Horizon, bedrock)   % r
%
% A word that is NOT one of these master horizons — `humus`, `loam`, `regolith`
% — is deliberately NOT a row, so a horizon recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a material. (Humus, for instance, is a component of soil organic
% matter, not itself a master horizon.) That honest-abstention property is what
% makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% horizon→material pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `material` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% word the source EXPLICITLY uses for that horizon — never one the source does
% not support. (Where the everyday word differs from the source's word — the
% source says the E horizon's dominant processes are "losses", not "leaching";
% it says the B horizon is a zone of "illuviation" — the atom uses the source's
% own word, so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every horizon→material pair
% below was read out of a fetched University of Nebraska–Lincoln passel Plant &
% Soil Sciences eLibrary lesson, and any pair whose material could not be
% verified there would have been dropped). Each pair, with the primary source it
% was copied from and the verbatim span that states it:
%
%   o → organic_matter
%     https://passel2.unl.edu/view/lesson/293965be23a0/3
%     (UNL passel — Plant & Soil Sciences eLibrary, Soil Genesis and Development)
%     "An O horizon has at least 20% organic matter by mass."
%
%   a → topsoil
%     https://passel2.unl.edu/view/lesson/293965be23a0/3
%     (UNL passel — Plant & Soil Sciences eLibrary, Soil Genesis and Development)
%     "An A horizon is a mineral horizon. This horizon always forms at the
%      surface and is what many people refer to as topsoil."
%
%   e → losses
%     https://passel2.unl.edu/view/lesson/293965be23a0/3
%     (UNL passel — Plant & Soil Sciences eLibrary, Soil Genesis and Development)
%     "The dominant processes of an E horizon are losses."
%
%   b → illuviation
%     https://passel2.unl.edu/view/lesson/293965be23a0/3
%     (UNL passel — Plant & Soil Sciences eLibrary, Soil Genesis and Development)
%     "A B horizon is typically a mineral subsurface horizon and is a zone of
%      accumulation, called illuviation."
%
%   c → parent_material
%     https://passel2.unl.edu/view/lesson/293965be23a0/3
%     (UNL passel — Plant & Soil Sciences eLibrary, Soil Genesis and Development)
%     "A C horizon consists of parent material, such as glacial till or lake
%      sediments that have little to no alteration due to the soil forming
%      processes."
%
%   r → bedrock
%     https://passel2.unl.edu/view/lesson/293965be23a0/3
%     (UNL passel — Plant & Soil Sciences eLibrary, Soil Genesis and Development)
%     "An R layer is bedrock."
%
% (The master-horizon framing itself is stated on the same lesson —
% "There are five soil horizons: O, A, E, B, and C." — with the R layer named
% separately as bedrock, another span of the same primary university teaching
% resource.)
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the passel statement that fixes
% the first row (o → organic_matter). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and
% verbatim span is listed above, so each material remains independently
% checkable. All sources are the UNL passel Plant & Soil Sciences eLibrary, a
% university soil-science teaching resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table horizon_material {
    columns horizon, material

    row (o, organic_matter)
    row (a, topsoil)
    row (e, losses)
    row (b, illuviation)
    row (c, parent_material)
    row (r, bedrock)

    source "An O horizon has at least 20% organic matter by mass."
    locator "https://passel2.unl.edu/view/lesson/293965be23a0/3"
    trust authoritative
}
